    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1215</span><span class="rf-text">Kublai is born, a son of Tolui and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorghaghtani-beki">Sorghaghtani Beki</a> and a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1253</span><span class="rf-text">Ordered by his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongke">Möngke</a> to attack <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a>, he takes the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dali-kingdom">Dali Kingdom</a> and spares its residents</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1260</span><span class="rf-text">A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurultai">kurultai</a> in his own territories proclaims him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-khan">Great Khan</a>, although another at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karakorum">Karakorum</a> has already named his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ariq-boke">Ariq Böke</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1264</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ariq-boke">Ariq Böke</a> surrenders to Kublai at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shangdu">Xanadu</a>, ending the civil war</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1271</span><span class="rf-text">Kublai officially creates the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a>, proclaiming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dadu">Dadu</a>, modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, the capital the following year</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1279</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> are destroyed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-yamen">Battle of Yamen</a>, where the last Song emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-bing-of-song">Zhao Bing</a> dies by jumping into the sea</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1281</span><span class="rf-text">The second invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> fails and its fleet is destroyed; his wife Chabi dies the same year</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1294</span><span class="rf-text">Kublai dies at the age of 78, having passed the seal of Crown Prince to his grandson Temür</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sorghaghtani-beki"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sorghaghtani-beki.jpg" alt="Sorghaghtani Beki" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sorghaghtani-beki">Sorghaghtani Beki</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TuluiWithQueenSorgaqtani.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rashid al-Din, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Kublai was born on 23 September 1215, the fourth son of Tolui and his second by his chief wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorghaghtani-beki">Sorghaghtani Beki</a>, and a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>. On his grandfather's advice his mother chose a Buddhist Tangut woman as his nurse, whom he later honoured highly. In 1224, near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ili-river">Ili River</a>, Genghis Khan performed a ceremony over his grandsons <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongke">Möngke</a> and Kublai after their first hunt, in which the nine-year-old Kublai had helped to kill a rabbit and an antelope. Genghis Khan died in 1227, and Tolui served as regent for two years until Kublai's uncle Ogedei was enthroned as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">Khagan</a> in 1229. Tolui died in 1232.</p><p>After the Mongol conquest of the Jin dynasty Ogedei in 1236 gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebei">Hebei</a> to the family of Tolui, and Kublai received an estate of his own with 10,000 households. Inexperienced, he gave local officials free rein; corruption and aggressive taxation drove large numbers of Han peasants to flee and tax revenues fell. Kublai came to his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> and ordered reforms, his mother sent new officials, the tax laws were revised and many of those who had fled returned.</p><p>The most prominent element of his early life was his study of, and strong attraction to, contemporary Han culture. He invited Haiyun, the leading Buddhist monk of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, to his ordo in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> and questioned him on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karakorum">Karakorum</a> in 1242; Haiyun named Kublai's son Zhenjin and introduced him to the monk Liu Bingzhong, a painter, calligrapher, poet and mathematician who became his adviser. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanxi">Shanxi</a> scholar Zhao Bi joined his entourage, as did men of other nationalities.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mongke"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mongke.jpg" alt="Möngke" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mongke">Möngke</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:M%C3%B6ngke_enthroned._Tarikh-i_Jahangushay-i_Juvaini,_1438,_Shiraz.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1438 artist, Shiraz, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>When his eldest brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongke">Möngke</a> became Khan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a> in 1251, Kublai received the viceroyalty over northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and moved his ordo to central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-mongolia">Inner Mongolia</a>. He raised the agricultural output of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a> and increased welfare spending, acts acclaimed by Han warlords. In 1253, ordered to attack <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a>, he took the Dali capital and spared its residents although the ruling Gao family had killed his envoys. Officials jealous of his success said he dreamed of an empire of his own, and in 1257 Möngke sent two inspectors who listed 142 breaches of regulations, executed some Han officials and abolished his pacification commission; Kublai appealed in person and Möngke publicly forgave him. In 1258 Kublai presided over a conference of Taoists and Buddhists, after which he forcibly converted 237 Taoist temples to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>.</p><p>Möngke died in 1259 while Kublai was marching to join him against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>. Kublai kept the death secret, besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuchang">Wuchang</a> and reached a peace agreement with the Song minister Jia Sidao. Warned by his wife that his younger brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ariq-boke">Ariq Böke</a> was raising troops, he went north and learned that a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurultai">kurultai</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karakorum">Karakorum</a> had named Ariq Böke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-khan">Great Khan</a> with the support of most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>'s descendants. Kublai summoned his own kurultai, less well attended, which proclaimed him Great Khan on 15 April 1260. In the war that followed Karakorum was destroyed, Kublai cut off its food supplies, and Ariq Böke surrendered at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shangdu">Xanadu</a> on 21 August 1264. Kublai pardoned him but executed his chief supporters.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marco-polo.jpg" alt="Marco Polo" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frontispice_%C3%A9dition_de_Nuremberg_1477_(cropped).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Kublai treated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> as his main base and adopted Chinese political and cultural models. In August 1260 he created a unified paper currency, convertible with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and gold. In 1271 he gave the Mongol regime in China the name Dai Yuan, and the following year proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dadu">Dadu</a>, known to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khanbaliq">Khanbaliq</a>, in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, the capital; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shangdu">Shangdu</a>, also called Xanadu, was his summer capital. Most of the domains were administered as provinces, each with a governor and vice-governor. He rebuilt the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-canal">Grand Canal</a>, repaired public buildings and extended highways, and the record of the Mongols lists 20,166 public schools created in his reign.</p><p>After the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiangyang">Xiangyang</a> in 1273, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trebuchet">trebuchets</a> of the siege engineers Ismail and Al al-Din were used, he made Bayan of the Baarin supreme commander against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>. The Song imperial family surrendered in 1276, and in 1279 the last Song loyalists were crushed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-yamen">Battle of Yamen</a>. Goryeo, invaded and made a tributary state in 1260, came under tighter control after 1273.</p><p>He appointed the Sakya lama Phagpa his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-preceptor">Imperial Preceptor</a>, with power over all the empire's Buddhist monks. Thirty Muslims served as high officials at his court and eight of the twelve administrative districts had Muslim governors; Muslim astronomers such as Jamal ad-Din introduced instruments that allowed the Chinese calendar to be corrected, while the astronomer and engineer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guo-shoujing">Guo Shoujing</a> built a 30 km channel to supply Dadu with water. Several Europeans visited the empire, notably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a> in the 1270s, who lived in the emperor's lands for 17 years.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ariq-boke"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ariq-boke.jpg" alt="Ariq Böke" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ariq-boke">Ariq Böke</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ariq_B%C3%B6ke.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Miskin, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Kublai's accession was contested from the start: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ariq-boke">Ariq Böke</a> had been elected by the representatives of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>'s four elder sons. Mongol traditionalists condemned his move to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khanbaliq">Khanbaliq</a> and his ties to Han culture, asking “What will happen to the old customs?”, and an uprising in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karakorum">Karakorum</a> was barely contained. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaidu">Kaidu</a> of the House of Ögedei, who refused to attend his court, declared himself the legitimate heir in place of a khan who had turned from the ways of Genghis Khan; he defeated Yuan armies, took Almaliq and briefly occupied Karakorum in 1289. In 1287 the prince Nayan, threatened by Kublai's bureaucratisation, revolted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>; Kublai led an army against him, Nayan was smothered under felt carpets, and the uprisings of his supporters were ruthlessly repressed until 1289.</p><p>The revolt of Li Tan in 1262 left Kublai distrustful of ethnic Han. In 1279–1280 he decreed death for those who slaughtered animals according to Islamic or Jewish law. His monetary policy ended badly: the bills issued from 1273 to finance the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> war became, through lack of fiscal discipline and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a>, an economic disaster, and gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> were confiscated from citizens and foreign merchants in exchange for notes.</p><p>Against the advice of some Confucian-trained advisers he invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>. The fleets sent to Japan in 1274 and 1281 were destroyed, and the Java expedition of 1293 withdrew with the loss of more than 3,000 troops. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> his grandson Buqa-Temür killed 10,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetans">Tibetans</a> in crushing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drikung-kagyu">Drikung Kagyu</a> in 1291.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After the death of his wife Chabi in 1281 Kublai began to withdraw from direct contact with his advisers and issued instructions through another of his queens, Nambui. His chosen successor was his son Zhenjin, who headed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-secretariat">Zhongshu Sheng</a> and administered the dynasty in Confucian fashion. In 1285 an official proposed that Kublai abdicate in Zhenjin's favour; the suggestion angered Kublai, who refused to see his son, and Zhenjin died soon afterwards, eight years before his father. Kublai regretted this and remained very close to Zhenjin's wife, Bairam, also known as Kokejin.</p><p>Kublai grew increasingly despondent after the deaths of his favourite wife and his heir, and the failure of the campaigns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> also haunted him. He turned to food and drink, became grossly overweight and suffered from gout and diabetes; his overindulgence in alcohol and the meat-rich Mongol diet may have contributed to the gout. He tried every treatment available, from Korean shamans to Vietnamese doctors, to no avail.</p><p>At the end of 1293 the emperor refused to take part in the traditional New Year's ceremony. Before his death he passed the seal of Crown Prince to Zhenjin's son Temür, who became the next <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">Khagan</a> and the second ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a>. The only old companion the palace staff could find to comfort him in his final illness was Bayan, more than 30 years his junior. Kublai weakened steadily and died on 18 February 1294 at the age of 78. Two days later the funeral cortège took his body to the burial place of the Khans in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/paper-money"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paper-money.jpg" alt="Paper money" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/paper-money">Paper money</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jiao_zi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Kublai's seizure of power in 1260 pushed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a> in a new direction. The controversy over his accession accelerated the disunity of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>: his real power was limited to the Yuan Empire, though as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">Khagan</a> he still influenced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> and, to a much lesser degree, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, and the Kublaids kept suzerainty over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhan">Ilkhans</a> until the end of their regime. His willingness to identify the Mongol-ruled realm as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> brought the empire to international attention, and his conquests and those of his predecessors were largely responsible for re-creating a unified, militarily powerful China. Yuan rule of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> from a capital at modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> set a precedent for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a>'s expansion into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-asia">Inner Asia</a>.</p><p>Under Kublai direct contact between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> was established, made possible by Mongol control of the Central Asian trade routes and by efficient postal services. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paper-money">paper money</a> was later imitated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaykhatu">Gaykhatu</a> of the Ilkhanate, a complete failure.</p><p>The invasions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> are judged differently: several scholars treat them as a success because <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tributary-system">tributary relations</a> were established, while Vietnamese historiography regards the war as a major victory over foreign invaders. Of the attempts on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, David Nicolle wrote that “the myth of Mongol invincibility had been shattered throughout eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>”; the maritime archaeologist Kenzo Hayashida, who found the wreckage of the second fleet, concluded that Kublai had rushed its construction.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Kublai Khan (1215–1294), grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>, was proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-khan">Great Khan</a> in 1260 and defeated his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ariq-boke">Ariq Böke</a> in a civil war that began the division of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a>. He founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a> in 1271 and by 1279 ruled all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. His invasions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> failed.</em></p>

